DESIGN DECISIONS, and why each is not the obvious choice

- The route takes an EXISTING paymentId instead of creating a row. Rent payments
  already arrive from the inbound-email webhook, so the common case is attaching
  a statement to a payment that is already recorded. Creating a row here would
  double-count the income.

- The route does NOT write `amount`. Only the confirm action does. An unconfirmed
  extraction replacing a recorded income figure would change reported income with
  nobody agreeing to it — and that figure may already have been corrected by
  hand, as two of the seven production rows were. The proposal is carried in
  `extracted` for the confirm step. Asserted by the check
  "amount NOT overwritten by the upload".

- The property is resolved THROUGH the payment with an RLS-filtered select, so a
  payment the caller cannot read is simply not found. Verified: attaching to
  another user's payment returns 404 and leaves that row untouched.

- Storage upload happens BEFORE extraction, so a failed extraction never loses
  the document. On a failed update the object is removed, because storage has no
  cascade and an orphan is the exact failure docs/account-deletion.md exists to
  prevent.

- Replacing a statement removes the previous object only AFTER the update
  succeeds, so a failure cannot destroy evidence that is still referenced.

- removeRentalStatement clears the statement fields but KEEPS the payment row.
  The row records income genuinely received; deleting a document is not a
  statement that the rent never arrived. Deleting the row would silently reduce
  reported income.

- confirmRentalStatement deliberately does NOT validate that the figures
  reconcile. A balance brought forward legitimately breaks the identity, and
  refusing the save would force the user to enter something false to get past it.
  The reconciliation is advisory per FR5. Recorded as a comment in the action.

